1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,698
Hi, I'm Shermaine Yap from Asia Pacific University in Malaysia. This is TakeTheFloor,

2
00:00:06,748 --> 00:00:11,761
my entry for the 305 EmpowHER hackathon, in the Analytics competition.

3
00:00:11,811 --> 00:00:16,679
TakeTheFloor shows who held the floor in a meeting, who got cut off,

4
00:00:16,729 --> 00:00:19,934
and whose ideas got credited to someone else.

5
00:00:19,984 --> 00:00:26,010
Then it gives every person private, Gemma-powered coaching to lead the next meeting.

6
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:29,120
This narration uses a text-to-speech voice.

7
00:00:30,670 --> 00:00:36,529
Leadership is won or lost in meetings, and research points to three patterns.

8
00:00:36,579 --> 00:00:39,752
First, airtime is uneven. In mixed groups,

9
00:00:39,802 --> 00:00:45,738
women's share of speaking time often falls below their share of seats. Second,

10
00:00:45,788 --> 00:00:47,963
interruptions are not random.

11
00:00:48,013 --> 00:00:54,179
Experimental work found that people interrupted women more often than men. Third,

12
00:00:54,229 --> 00:00:58,093
ideas lose their author. Someone repeats your idea,

13
00:00:58,143 --> 00:01:00,779
and the room gives them the credit.

14
00:01:00,829 --> 00:01:05,460
None of this shows up in meeting notes, so nobody can fix it.

15
00:01:07,010 --> 00:01:11,171
Here is the moment I built this for. In this demo meeting,

16
00:01:11,221 --> 00:01:15,601
Aisha proposes a student pricing tier, and Mark cuts her off.

17
00:01:15,651 --> 00:01:20,465
Two minutes later, Mark pitches the same idea almost word for word,

18
00:01:20,515 --> 00:01:25,476
and Daniel says: great idea, Mark, let's go with Mark's pricing plan.

19
00:01:25,526 --> 00:01:28,380
TakeTheFloor catches this automatically.

20
00:01:29,930 --> 00:01:34,281
The flow is simple. You drop in a transcript from Zoom, Teams,

21
00:01:34,331 --> 00:01:40,882
Meet or Otter. Transparent detectors measure airtime, interruptions, hedging and idea credit,

22
00:01:40,932 --> 00:01:46,276
and every flag carries its evidence. Then Gemma 4 adds judgement in context,

23
00:01:46,326 --> 00:01:51,032
and every person gets private coaching, built from their own words.

24
00:01:51,082 --> 00:01:53,090
Let me show you the live app.

25
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:58,519
This is the live app, with the product launch meeting loaded.

26
00:01:58,569 --> 00:02:02,383
At the top, Floor Health is thirty-eight out of one hundred.

27
00:02:02,433 --> 00:02:06,891
It combines airtime balance with interruptions and mis-credited ideas.

28
00:02:06,941 --> 00:02:11,850
There are four real interruptions, and one idea credited to the wrong person.

29
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:16,423
The card shows the evidence: Aisha's original words with the timestamp,

30
00:02:16,473 --> 00:02:21,060
Mark's repeat two minutes later, and the moment the credit went to Mark.

31
00:02:23,618 --> 00:02:28,512
Below that is airtime. Mark held forty-seven percent of the talk time,

32
00:02:28,562 --> 00:02:32,397
more than twice a fair share, while Aisha and Mei Ling,

33
00:02:32,447 --> 00:02:36,352
who brought the pricing and data ideas, spoke the least.

34
00:02:36,402 --> 00:02:38,965
Every chart has a plain text summary,

35
00:02:39,015 --> 00:02:41,508
and a table view for screen readers.

36
00:02:44,067 --> 00:02:46,557
The facilitator gets structural fixes,

37
00:02:46,607 --> 00:02:50,433
like a round-robin for decisions and a let-them-land norm,

38
00:02:50,483 --> 00:02:54,978
because the goal is to change the meeting, not to blame individuals.

39
00:02:55,028 --> 00:02:58,787
The full report downloads as an accessible Markdown file.

40
00:03:01,339 --> 00:03:07,611
The floor timeline shows every turn on one time axis. Triangles mark interruptions.

41
00:03:07,661 --> 00:03:10,886
The diamond is where Aisha raised her idea,

42
00:03:10,936 --> 00:03:13,857
and the star is where Mark repeated it.

43
00:03:13,907 --> 00:03:17,589
Hovering over a turn shows exactly what was said.

44
00:03:20,150 --> 00:03:23,791
The interruptions tab shows who interrupted whom.

45
00:03:23,841 --> 00:03:27,031
Mark cut off Aisha twice and Mei Ling once,

46
00:03:27,081 --> 00:03:29,140
and Daniel cut off Mei Ling.

47
00:03:31,698 --> 00:03:34,059
But not every overlap is rude,

48
00:03:34,109 --> 00:03:38,318
so each moment is labelled by Gemma in context. Here,

49
00:03:38,368 --> 00:03:43,621
Daniel just says yeah. That's a backchannel, so it is not counted.

50
00:03:43,671 --> 00:03:48,282
And here, Aisha says: yes, and, building on Priya's point.

51
00:03:48,332 --> 00:03:54,148
That's a supportive overlap. This way, friendly energy is never punished.

52
00:03:56,712 --> 00:03:59,870
The idea credit tracker shows the full chain.

53
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:01,866
Gemma gave the idea a title,

54
00:04:01,916 --> 00:04:07,142
and the shared key terms and similarity score keep every flag explainable.

55
00:04:09,701 --> 00:04:15,407
The language tab measures hedging, like pre-apologies, and self-discounting such as:

56
00:04:15,457 --> 00:04:17,531
this might be a dumb idea, but.

57
00:04:17,581 --> 00:04:20,409
Each hedge comes with a confident rewrite,

58
00:04:20,459 --> 00:04:23,561
so a strong idea sounds like a recommendation.

59
00:04:26,117 --> 00:04:29,345
This is the private leadership coach. For Aisha,

60
00:04:29,395 --> 00:04:32,760
Gemma highlights real strengths from this meeting,

61
00:04:32,810 --> 00:04:35,560
gives three concrete moves for next time,

62
00:04:35,610 --> 00:04:38,155
and scripts she can say word for word,

63
00:04:38,205 --> 00:04:41,297
like reclaiming the floor when she is cut off.

64
00:04:43,853 --> 00:04:45,112
Switching to Priya,

65
00:04:45,162 --> 00:04:50,762
Gemma rewrites her own hedged sentences into confident ones that keep her meaning.

66
00:04:50,812 --> 00:04:55,723
These notes download for that person only. It is never a public ranking.

67
00:04:58,275 --> 00:05:04,563
The accessible recap is written in plain language for teammates who are deaf or hard of hearing,

68
00:05:04,613 --> 00:05:09,316
neurodivergent, or working in a second language. It lists the decisions,

69
00:05:09,366 --> 00:05:11,825
who does what, and the open questions.

70
00:05:14,377 --> 00:05:17,984
The captions tab lists every turn with its speaker,

71
00:05:18,034 --> 00:05:22,717
and writes every event out in words, so nothing depends on colour.

72
00:05:25,269 --> 00:05:32,100
Accessibility is built in. Here I switch on the dyslexia-friendly Atkinson Hyperlegible font,

73
00:05:32,150 --> 00:05:33,802
and high contrast mode.

74
00:05:33,852 --> 00:05:38,759
Names can also be anonymised before results are shared with a team.

75
00:05:41,315 --> 00:05:45,356
Here is a second meeting, a university project group.

76
00:05:45,406 --> 00:05:51,223
Nurul suggests analysing LRT ridership early on and gets interrupted. Later,

77
00:05:51,273 --> 00:05:55,700
Wei Jie pitches the same topic, and the group credits him.

78
00:05:55,750 --> 00:06:01,026
Notice the green card too. Kavitha builds on Jason's food price idea,

79
00:06:01,076 --> 00:06:06,815
and names him. That is the healthy pattern, and TakeTheFloor celebrates it.

80
00:06:09,374 --> 00:06:14,966
Finally, the control meeting. In this healthy stand-up, Floor Health is eighty-six,

81
00:06:15,016 --> 00:06:16,801
there are no interruptions,

82
00:06:16,851 --> 00:06:20,811
and Ravi credits Grace by name when he builds on her audit.

83
00:06:20,861 --> 00:06:23,734
TakeTheFloor does not just flag everything.

84
00:06:26,293 --> 00:06:30,339
For the analytics, I wrote three meetings with hand labels,

85
00:06:30,389 --> 00:06:32,491
including that healthy control.

86
00:06:32,541 --> 00:06:38,808
The detectors found five out of five interruptions and four out of four idea credit events,

87
00:06:38,858 --> 00:06:43,945
with zero false alarms, and Gemma agreed with all six interruption labels.

88
00:06:43,995 --> 00:06:51,096
A sensitivity sweep in my Kaggle notebook shows the result is stable across a wide range of thresholds.

89
00:06:51,146 --> 00:06:52,831
To be honest about scope:

90
00:06:52,881 --> 00:06:56,997
this shows the method behaves as designed on synthetic data.

91
00:06:57,047 --> 00:06:59,843
It is not a claim of real-world accuracy.

92
00:07:01,393 --> 00:07:06,042
Accessibility is a feature, not a checkbox: a plain-language recap,

93
00:07:06,092 --> 00:07:10,811
readable fonts and contrast, charts that never rely on colour alone,

94
00:07:10,861 --> 00:07:12,003
and safe sharing.

95
00:07:13,553 --> 00:07:17,611
It is responsible by default. It can run fully offline,

96
00:07:17,661 --> 00:07:21,869
or with a local Gemma, so transcripts stay on the device.

97
00:07:21,919 --> 00:07:26,201
It describes one meeting, and never grades a person. Next,

98
00:07:26,251 --> 00:07:29,562
I want to validate on the AMI meeting corpus,

99
00:07:29,612 --> 00:07:32,102
add a live nudge for facilitators,

100
00:07:32,152 --> 00:07:36,733
and an amplify button to credit a colleague's idea in one tap.

101
00:07:38,283 --> 00:07:42,981
TakeTheFloor makes the invisible visible, so every voice gets heard,

102
00:07:43,031 --> 00:07:45,075
and gets the credit. The code,

103
00:07:45,125 --> 00:07:49,753
the notebook and the live app are all linked on Devpost. Thank you.
